---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const naclCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'Na ([Ne] 3s¹) → Na⁺ + e⁻', note: 'Sodium spends almost nothing to shed its one outer electron, exposing a stable full shell.' },
    { math: 'Cl ([Ne] 3s² 3p⁵) + e⁻ → Cl⁻', note: 'Chlorine is one electron short of a full shell — and grabs this one hard (electronegativity 3.16).' },
    { math: 'Na⁺ + Cl⁻ → NaCl', note: 'Opposite charges attract; each ion ends up surrounded by six of the other kind in a cubic lattice.' },
    { math: 'lattice energy ≈ −787 kJ/mol', note: 'That released energy is the reason salt forms at all — and why melting it takes real heat (801 °C).' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/chemical-bonding">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Atoms bond for one reason: full outer shells are stable, and sharing or transferring electrons
    gets them there. Three strategies dominate. <strong>Ionic bonds</strong> transfer an electron
    outright (metal gives, non-metal takes). <strong>Covalent bonds</strong> share a pair.
    <strong>Metallic bonds</strong> pool electrons into a common "sea" owned by no atom in
    particular.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="table salt, derived in four steps — the classic ionic hand-off" widget="equationStep" config={naclCfg} />

  <h2>Telling the three apart</h2>
  <p>
    Electronegativity difference is the practical test: over ~1.7 → ionic; ~0.4–1.7 → polar
    covalent (shared, but unevenly — this is why water is bent and dissolves salt); under ~0.4 →
    pure covalent (equal partners, like O₂).
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ionic compounds</strong> (NaCl, MgO) are crystalline, brittle, high-melting, and conduct electricity only when dissolved or molten — the ions must be free to move.</li>
    <li><strong>Covalent molecules</strong> (H₂O, CO₂, CH₄) are the opposite: often gases or liquids, low melting points, poor conductors. Diamond is the great exception — one giant covalent network, hence its hardness.</li>
    <li><strong>Metals</strong> conduct and bend because that electron sea flows: the "free electrons" in a wire are literally the metallic bond carrying current.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Bonding is a spectrum, not three boxes — every bond is part sharing, part stealing. The labels
    are shorthand for where a bond sits on that line.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Chemical Bonding); CRC Handbook (lattice energies, melting points).</p>
</TopicPage>
